Sec6
6 Shear The shear tensor is the symmetric and traceless part of a tensor \(u_{\mu ;\rho }(\delta ^\rho _\nu -u^\rho u_\nu )\) , where \(u^{\mu }\) is the four-velocity and the semicolon denotes covariant differentiation with respect to the Levi-Civita connection [ 31 ]. If the four-acceleration vanishes, \(w_\mu =u^\nu u_{\mu ;\nu }=0\) , then the shear tensor is equal to $$\begin{aligned} \sigma _{\mu \nu }=u_{(\mu ;\nu )}-\frac{1}{3}u^\rho {}_{;\rho }(g_{\mu \nu }-u_\mu u_\nu ). \end{aligned}$$ (23) Using \(u_{\mu ;\nu }=u_{\mu ,\nu }-\Gamma ^{\ \ \rho }_{\mu \ \nu }u_\rho \) leads to the nonzero components of this tensor: $$\begin{aligned} \sigma ^{1}_1=\frac{2}{3}\Bigl (\frac{\dot{X}}{X}-\frac{\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr ),\quad \sigma ^{2}_{2}=\sigma ^{3}_{3}=\frac{1}{3}\Bigl (\frac{\dot{Y}}{Y}-\frac{\dot{X}}{X}\Bigr ). \end{aligned}$$ (24) The shear scalar is $$\begin{aligned} \sigma ^{2}=\frac{1}{2}\sigma ^{\mu }_\nu \sigma ^\nu _\mu =\frac{1}{3}\Bigl (\frac{\dot{X}}{X}-\frac{\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr )^{2}. \end{aligned}$$ (25) Accordingly, the shear vanishes and the universe is isotropic if X is proportional to Y . Using the parametric equations for the scale factors gives the shear scalar as a function of \(\eta \) . It diverges at \(\eta =0\) for vacuum, and at \(\eta =\pi /2\) . The expansion scalar is the trace part: $$\begin{aligned} \theta =u^{\rho }{}_{;\rho }=\Gamma ^{\ \ \rho }_{\rho \ \nu }u^{\nu }=\frac{\dot{X}}{X}+\frac{2\dot{Y}}{Y}=3H. \end{aligned}$$ (26) The rotation tensor \(\omega _{\mu \nu }\) is the antisymmetric part of \(u_{\mu ;\rho }(\delta ^\rho _\nu -u^\rho u_\nu )\) and vanishes for any values of X and Y . The rate of the first component of the shear tensor is $$\begin{aligned} \dot{\sigma }^{1}_1=\frac{2}{3}\Bigl (\frac{X\ddot{X}-\dot{X}^{2}}{X^{2}} -\frac{Y\ddot{Y}-\dot{Y}^{2}}{Y^{2}}\Bigr ). \end{aligned}$$ (27) Using this equation with ( 11 ) and ( 16 ) gives $$\begin{aligned} \dot{\sigma }^{1}_1+3H\sigma ^{1}_1=\frac{2}{3Y^{2}}. \end{aligned}$$ (28) Similarly, the rates of the other components satisfy $$\begin{aligned} \dot{\sigma }^{2}_{2}+3H\sigma ^{2}_{2}=\dot{\sigma }^{3}_{3}+3H\sigma ^{3}_{3} =-\frac{1}{3Y^{2}}. \end{aligned}$$ (29) Consequently, the rate of the shear scalar \(d(\sigma ^{2})/dt=\dot{\sigma }^{\mu }_\nu \sigma ^\nu _\mu \) satisfies $$\begin{aligned} \frac{d(\sigma ^{2})}{dt}+6H\sigma ^{2}=\frac{2}{3Y^{2}}\Bigl (\frac{\dot{X}}{X} -\frac{\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr ). \end{aligned}$$ (30) The right-hand side in this equation is positive, which indicates that the shear scalar grows with decreasing a faster than \(\sim a^{-6}\) .

Sec7
7 Avoidance of singularity The Raychaudhuri equation for a congruence of geodesics without four-acceleration and rotation is [ 31 ] $$\begin{aligned} \frac{d\theta }{ds}=-\frac{1}{3}\theta ^{2}-2(\sigma ^{2}-\omega ^{2}) +w^{\mu }{}_{;\mu }-R_{\mu \nu }u^{\mu } u^\nu , \end{aligned}$$ (31) where \(\omega ^{2}=\omega _{\mu \nu }\omega ^{\mu \nu }/2\) is the rotation scalar. In the KS spacetime in comoving coordinates, \(\omega ^{2}=w^{\mu }{}_{;\mu }=0\) . For a spin fluid, the last term in ( 31 ) is equal to \(-\kappa (\tilde{\epsilon }+3\tilde{p})/2\) . Consequently, the necessary and sufficient condition for avoiding a singularity in a black hole is \(-\kappa (\tilde{\epsilon }+3\tilde{p})/2>2\sigma ^{2}\) . For a relativistic spin fluid, \(p=\epsilon /3\) , this condition is equivalent to $$\begin{aligned} 2\kappa \alpha n_{\text {f}}^{2}>2\sigma ^{2}+\kappa \epsilon . \end{aligned}$$ (32) Without torsion, the left-hand side of ( 32 ) would be absent and this inequality would not be satisfied, resulting in a singularity. Consequently, torsion provides a mechanism for preventing a singularity. However, this mechanism alone is not sufficient. If the number of fermions in a black hole is constant, then the fermion number density grows with decreasing a according to \(\sim a^{-3}\) . Since the shear scalar \(\sigma ^{2}\) grows with decreasing a faster than \(\sim a^{-6}\) , \(n_{\text {f}}^{2}\) growing as \(\sim a^{-6}\) could not satisfy ( 32 ) at sufficiently small values of a and a singularity would form. Therefore, to avoid a singularity, \(n_{\text {f}}^{2}\) must grow faster and fermions must be produced in a black hole. Quantum particle creation in strongly changing gravitational fields can provide a mechanism that ensures that torsion dominates over shear in the Raychaudhuri equation. Consequently, torsion and particle production together can replace a singularity in a universe in a black hole with a nonsingular bounce.

Sec8
8 Particle production The interior of a forming black hole would become a vacuum KS universe when all the matter in the black hole reached the central singularity. However, quantum effects in changing gravitational fields cause particle-pair production that replaces vacuum with a relativistic spin fluid. These effects may eliminate the singularity and replace it with a nonsingular bounce. The production rate of particles in a contracting or expanding universe can be phenomenologically given by $$\begin{aligned} \frac{1}{\sqrt{-g}}\frac{d(\sqrt{-g}n_{\text {f}})}{dt}=\beta H^{4}, \end{aligned}$$ (33) where \(g=-X^{2}Y^{4}=-a^6\) is the determinant of the metric tensor in ( 6 ) and \(\beta \) is the production rate [ 14 , 15 ]. Accordingly, $$\begin{aligned} \dot{n}_{\text {f}}+3Hn_{\text {f}}=\beta H^{4},\quad \frac{d(n^{2}_{\text {f}})}{dt} +6Hn^{2}_{\text {f}}=2\beta n_{\text {f}}H^{4}. \end{aligned}$$ (34) To avoid a singularity, the rate of \(n^{2}_{\text {f}}\) must exceed the rate of \(\sigma ^{2}\) . Comparing ( 34 ) with ( 30 ) gives $$\begin{aligned} \frac{2\beta n_{\text {f}}}{81}\Bigl (\frac{\dot{X}}{X} +\frac{2\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr )^{4}>\frac{2}{3Y^{2}}\Bigl (\frac{\dot{X}}{X} -\frac{\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr ). \end{aligned}$$ (35) After the formation of the event horizon, at the instant when ( 32 ) is reached, this inequality must be satisfied to ensure that ( 32 ) continues to hold.

Sec9
9 Dynamics of universe with spin fluid The spin fluid in the early universe is formed by an ultrarelativistic matter in kinetic equilibrium, for which \(\epsilon =h_\star T^{4}\) , \(p=\epsilon /3\) , and \(n_{\text {f}}=h_{n{\text {f}}}T^{3}\) , where T is the temperature of the universe, \(h_\star =(\pi ^{2}/30)(g_\text {b}+(7/8)g_{\text {f}})k_\text {B}^{4}/(\hbar c)^{3}\) , and \(h_{n{\text {f}}}=(\zeta (3)/\pi ^{2})(3/4)g_{\text {f}}k_\text {B}^{3}/(\hbar c)^{3}\) [ 14 , 15 , 52 ]. For standard-model particles, \(g_{\text {b}}=29\) and \(g_{\text {f}}=90\) . In the presence of spin and torsion, the first field equation in ( 10 ) with ( 1 ) becomes $$\begin{aligned} \frac{\dot{Y}^{2}+1}{Y^{2}}+\frac{2\dot{X}\dot{Y}}{XY}=\kappa (h_\star T^{4} -\alpha h_{n{\text {f}}}^{2} T^6). \end{aligned}$$ (36) Without particle production, ( 1 ) and ( 14 ) give the constancy of \(XY^{2} T^{3}\) , which is equivalent to \(\dot{T}/T+H=0\) . With particle production, this constancy is replaced with a relation that follows from the first equation in ( 34 ): $$\begin{aligned} \frac{\dot{T}}{T}+H=\frac{\beta H^{4}}{3h_{n{\text {f}}}T^{3}}. \end{aligned}$$ (37) Equations ( 36 ) and ( 37 ), with ( 11 ) and ( 16 ), determine the time dependence of X , Y , and T . The only parameters are the mass M of the parent black hole that created the universe and the production rate \(\beta \) . The initial conditions at \(t=0\) for these equations could be taken as those for the dynamics for the interior of a black hole becoming a KS universe with dust: \(X_{0}=\kappa m\) , \(Y_{0}=2GM\) , \(\dot{X}_{0}=-B/(4GM)\) , and \(\dot{Y}_{0}=0\) . The coordinate r in ( 6 ) can be rescaled to remove the arbitrariness of the constant m . Consequently, the only arbitrary initial condition is the initial value of \(\dot{X}\) , related to the constant B , which represents the radial velocity of the collapsing matter when the event horizon forms. If the parent black hole were rotating, then it would be described by the Kerr metric [ 47 , 109 ] and the angular momentum of the black hole would be another parameter. The interior of a Kerr black hole would not be represented by the KS metric; it would be a more general, anisotropic universe. If \(\beta \) is big enough, then particle production will increase T quickly enough to make the right-hand side of ( 36 ) negative while keeping the left-hand side positive. This increase will prevent a cosmological singularity and generate a bounce. After the bounce in Y , the universe expands and Y reaches values larger than 2 GM . Since the universe is closed, Y eventually stops increasing and starts decreasing, and the universe contracts to another bounce. Because of particle production, which increases entropy, each cycle of expansion and contraction lasts longer and reaches a larger maximum of Y than the preceding cycle [ 14 , 15 , 74 , 75 ]. A similar behavior occurs for a . The universe may have several bounces and cycles until it reaches the size at which the cosmological constant becomes dominant and prevents the next contraction. Then the cycles end and the universe begins indefinite expansion [ 14 , 15 , 74 , 75 ]. The last bounce would be the big bang.

Sec11
11 Summary The closed geometry [ 1 ] and anisotropy [ 110 , 111 , 112 ] of the observed universe may indicate that the universe was born in a black hole. We analyzed a closed Kantowski–Sachs universe satisfying the Einstein–Cartan field equations with a spin fluid. We showed that torsion and particle production may together violate the strong energy condition and give the universe one or more nonsingular bounces. Furthermore, particle production in an expanding phase can generate a finite period of inflation, during which the universe produces enormous amounts of matter and entropy, and isotropizes. This scenario is only approximate. Its purpose was to show how torsion and particle production could avoid a singularity even if the shear is present. In reality, the interior Schwarzschild metric and therefore the KS metric, which has topology \(R\times S^{2}\) , is never reached if a nonsingular bounce occurs. It should be replaced with a more general Bianchi IX form, that could allow the universe to asymptotically tend to the closed FLRW geometry with topology \(S^{3}\) .

